WEBVTT

00:03.760 --> 00:06.600
All right, friends, now let's do a recap about the case statements.

00:06.640 --> 00:12.480
Case statements can go and evaluate a list of conditions one by one, and return a value once the first

00:12.480 --> 00:13.400
condition is met.

00:13.400 --> 00:17.920
And if you are talking about the rules of using the case statements, we have only one where the data

00:17.960 --> 00:22.200
types of each condition after the then and else must be matching.

00:22.240 --> 00:28.240
And now if we talk about the use cases of the case statements, the main use case is to do data transformations

00:28.240 --> 00:32.040
and especially by creating new columns and deriving new informations.

00:32.040 --> 00:35.680
So as we saw, there are amazing use cases for the case statements.

00:35.680 --> 00:38.360
For example, we can use it in order to categorize our data.

00:38.400 --> 00:43.520
As we learned, we can go and create new groups of data then to be aggregated for our reports.

00:43.520 --> 00:46.360
And then we saw another use case is mapping values.

00:46.360 --> 00:52.440
We can use the case statement in order to help us mapping the critical technical values that are stored

00:52.440 --> 00:57.400
in databases to new values, which is more readable and more friendly to be used.

00:57.400 --> 01:00.560
And the next use case that we have learned is handling the nulls.

01:00.560 --> 01:06.280
We can use the case statement in order to replace the nulls with value to make our aggregations more

01:06.280 --> 01:06.920
accurate.

01:06.920 --> 01:12.240
And the last use case that we have learned, and I think the most used one in my project is doing conditional

01:12.240 --> 01:18.280
aggregations, where we can aggregate a subset of data that meets specific conditions in order to do

01:18.320 --> 01:20.200
focus and target analysis.

01:20.240 --> 01:25.280
Okay my friends, so with that, we have covered all the topics and all the functions in order to transform

01:25.280 --> 01:28.320
a single value in SQL, the row level functions.

01:28.320 --> 01:31.520
That was very important, especially for data engineers.

01:31.520 --> 01:32.960
So we are done with this chapter.

01:33.000 --> 01:35.560
Now we are moving to very interesting chapter.

01:35.560 --> 01:38.360
Finally we can talk about data analytics in SQL.

01:38.360 --> 01:43.400
And we will be covering now the aggregate and the analytical functions that we have in SQL.

01:43.440 --> 01:45.320
So first we're going to start with the basics.

01:45.320 --> 01:48.200
So we will learn simple functions on how to aggregate your data.

01:48.200 --> 01:49.040
So let's go.
